---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Worth Running';
const description = 'Which models are worth running: capability against system requirements, for one user and for many, and how the state-of-the-art quants change the cost.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} nav="worth">
  <main class="wrap" data-pagefind-body>
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}>index</a> / <span>worth running</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>Worth Running</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        Models ranked by capability against what they actually need. Two numbers
        that are usually conflated, kept apart here. <strong>Resident</strong> is the
        whole checkpoint at <strong>4-bit</strong> — what has to fit somewhere in
        VRAM plus system RAM. <strong>Read / token</strong> is only the weights the
        decode step touches, which is what speed scales with and is far smaller for a
        mixture-of-experts model: a 600B MoE reads 13.5 GB per token and still needs
        a 279 GB file. <strong>Need</strong> adds the KV cache for
        <strong>one user</strong> or for <strong>16 concurrent users</strong> at an
        8k context, the part that grows with users. <em>Offload</em> marks models
        that stay fast when the routed experts sit in system RAM.
      </p>
      <p class="sub">
        <strong>Worth now</strong> is not a raw average, and it is not a plain
        relative score either. Capability is moving fast enough that recency has to
        dominate, so it is applied twice. First, a benchmark only counts as far as
        it still <em>separates current models</em>: its weight is
        <code>1 - share of reporters within 5 points of the catalog best</code>,
        multiplied by the age of its leaders — 2026-led 1.0, 2025-led 0.30,
        2024-led 0.06, older 0. MMLU-CoT, HellaSwag and 8-shot GSM8K now carry
        nothing; HLE, SWE-bench Pro and Terminal-Bench drive the order. Second, the
        model itself ages: the capability score is multiplied by 1.0 for a 2026
        release, 0.90 for 2025, 0.70 for 2024, 0.50 for 2023, and lower before that.
        In between, each benchmark is scored against the best result recorded for
        that same benchmark here, so models tested on different suites stay
        comparable, and the score is shrunk toward the catalog median in proportion
        to how little evidence stands behind it — a model carried by one axis cannot
        top the list on a technicality. <strong>cap</strong> under each score is that
        pre-recency capability with its evidence weight, and the tooltip carries the
        unshrunk number. <strong>thin</strong> marks a ranking resting on one or two
        axes, <strong>vendor</strong> marks scores the lab published about itself, and
        <strong>Year</strong> is the release year the recency factor is computed from.
      </p>
      <p class="sub">
        Scores are the model at its <strong>default serving configuration</strong>,
        not its ceiling. This matters where a lab publishes several reasoning
        levels: gpt-oss sets <code>reasoning_effort</code> to <code>medium</code>
        when you do not pass one, and its published <code>high</code> column is a
        test-time-compute ceiling — ranking on that put gpt-oss-20b above models it
        does not actually beat. Where a source documents the setting it is recorded
        on the entry; where it publishes only a ceiling, the record says so.
      </p>
      <p class="sub">
        <strong>Quant kept</strong> is the one column that is <em>not</em> a lab claim. It is
        the median gap between some third party's quantized build of the model and that
        build's own base, read off the derivative repo's comparison table and recomputed with
        the metric's direction applied. Only <strong data-quant-cov>some</strong> models have such a
        build published; the rest show a dash rather than a guess. Within
        <strong>±1%</strong> is noise, <strong>−1 to −5%</strong> is a real haircut, worse
        than <strong>−5%</strong> is a different and dumber model. Hover a value for the repo
        it came from — card-sourced, not a catalog record.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="join-controls" data-worth-controls>
      <div class="control">
        <label for="w-fit">Fits in</label>
        <select id="w-fit" data-filter="fit">
          <option value="">anything</option>
          <option value="16">16 GB</option>
          <option value="24">24 GB</option>
          <option value="48">48 GB</option>
          <option value="64">64 GB</option>
          <option value="128">128 GB</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="control">
        <label for="w-users">Users</label>
        <select id="w-users" data-filter="users">
          <option value="single">one</option>
          <option value="multi">16 concurrent</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="control">
        <label for="w-offload">Offload</label>
        <select id="w-offload" data-filter="offload">
          <option value="">all</option>
          <option value="friendly">stays fast in RAM</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="control">
        <label for="w-scored">Show</label>
        <select id="w-scored" data-filter="scored">
          <option value="scored">scored only</option>
          <option value="">including unscored</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <span class="control-count" data-count></span>
    </div>

    <div class="cost-table-wrap">
      <table class="cost-table" data-worth-table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Model</th>
            <th class="num">Year</th>
            <th class="num">Worth now<br><span class="th-note">recency-weighted</span></th>
            <th class="num">Active</th>
            <th class="num">Resident</th>
            <th class="num">Read / token</th>
            <th class="num">KV / user</th>
            <th class="num">Need</th>
            <th class="num">Quant kept<br><span class="th-note">measured</span></th>
            <th>Offload</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody data-worth-body></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What the quant changes</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The same weights at a lower bit-width. State-of-the-art schemes hold quality
      well past naive 4-bit, which is what moves a model from "needs a datacenter"
      to "fits one card". Quality retention is against the lab's own full-precision
      baseline and is a vendor claim.
    </p>
    <div class="cost-table-wrap">
      <table class="cost-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Bits</th><th>Quality kept</th><th>Where it runs</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>GSQ-RCO (ISTA-DASLab)</td><td>3.5</td>
            <td>task-lossless vs the base</td><td>stock llama.cpp GGUF</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>NVFP4 / MXFP4</td><td>4</td>
            <td>within a point of FP16 on reasoning</td><td>Blackwell native; emulated elsewhere</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>QAT 2-bit (Nemotron 3.5 Bolt)</td><td>2</td>
            <td>beats larger 16-bit models on the lab's table</td><td>24 GB, vLLM nightly</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ternary (Bonsai)</td><td>1.72</td>
            <td>98% of FP16 benchmark average, vendor claim</td><td>needs a forked llama.cpp</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  interface Bench { name: string; pct: number; vendor_claim: boolean; }
  interface Row {
    id: string; name: string; active_params_b: number; total_params_b: number | null;
    weights_q4_gib: number; traffic_q4_gib: number; kv_8k_gib: number | null; single_user_gib: number;
    multi_user_gib: number | null; offload_friendly: boolean;
    capability: number | null; benchmarks: Bench[]; vendor_only: boolean;
    bench_count?: number; thin?: boolean; release_year?: number | null;
    evidence_weight?: number; raw_capability?: number | null; ranked_axes?: number;
    worth?: number; recency_factor?: number;
    quant_retention?: {
      id: string; format: string | null; bucket: string;
      median_delta_pct: number; rows: number;
    } | null;
    quant_retention_basis?: string;
  }

  const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
  const tbody = document.querySelector('[data-worth-body]') as HTMLElement;
  let rows: Row[] = [];

  function need(r: Row, users: string): number | null {
    return users === 'multi' ? r.multi_user_gib : r.single_user_gib;
  }

  function esc(s: string): string {
    return s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;').replace(/"/g, '&quot;');
  }

  // Measured retention, read from a derivative repo's own base-vs-quant table.
  // Negative always means the quant scored below its base (PPL/KLD sign already applied upstream).
  function quantCell(r: Row): string {
    const q = r.quant_retention;
    if (!q) return '<td class="num">—</td>';
    const d = q.median_delta_pct;
    const cls = d <= -5 ? 'quant-bad' : d <= -1 ? 'quant-mid' : 'quant-good';
    const label = `${d > 0 ? '+' : ''}${d.toFixed(1)}%`;
    const tip = `${q.id} — ${q.format ?? 'quant'} vs its base, median of ${q.rows} benchmark${q.rows === 1 ? '' : 's'} (${q.bucket}). Card-sourced, not a record claim.`;
    return `<td class="num ${cls}" title="${esc(tip)}">${label}<div class="bench-line">${esc(q.format ?? '')}</div></td>`;
  }

  function render() {
    const fit = (document.querySelector('[data-filter="fit"]') as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const users = (document.querySelector('[data-filter="users"]') as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const offload = (document.querySelector('[data-filter="offload"]') as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const scored = (document.querySelector('[data-filter="scored"]') as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const cap = fit ? Number(fit) : Infinity;

    const shown = rows.filter((r) => {
      if (scored === 'scored' && (r.worth ?? null) === null) return false;
      if (offload === 'friendly' && !r.offload_friendly) return false;
      const n = need(r, users);
      if (n === null || n > cap) return false;
      return true;
    });

    tbody.innerHTML = shown.map((r) => {
      const n = need(r, users);
      const benches = r.benchmarks.map((b) => `${b.name} ${b.relative ?? b.pct}`).join(' · ');
      const cov = r.bench_count ? `${r.bench_count} ${r.bench_count === 1 ? 'test' : 'tests'} · ` : '';
      const flag = r.vendor_only ? ' <span class="vendor-flag">vendor</span>' : '';
      return `<tr class="model-row">
        <td><a href="${base}r/models/${r.id}/">${r.name}</a>${benches ? `<div class="bench-line">${cov}${benches}</div>` : ''}</td>
        <td class="num">${r.release_year ?? '—'}</td>
        <td class="num cost-cell">${(r.worth ?? null) === null ? '—' : r.worth.toFixed(1)}${r.evidence_weight ? `<div class="bench-line" title="capability ${r.capability} x recency ${r.recency_factor} (${r.release_year ?? 'undated'}); evidence weight ${r.evidence_weight} across ${r.ranked_axes} axes; unshrunk ${r.raw_capability ?? '—'}">cap ${r.capability}</div>` : ''}${r.thin ? ' <span class="thin-flag" title="only one or two axes carry discriminating evidence">thin</span>' : ''}${flag}</td>
        <td class="num">${r.active_params_b}B</td>
        <td class="num">${r.weights_q4_gib} GB</td>
        <td class="num">${r.traffic_q4_gib} GB</td>
        <td class="num">${r.kv_8k_gib === null ? '—' : r.kv_8k_gib + ' GB'}</td>
        <td class="num cost-cell">${n === null ? '—' : n + ' GB'}</td>
        ${quantCell(r)}
        <td>${r.offload_friendly ? 'yes' : '—'}</td>
      </tr>`;
    }).join('');

    const counter = document.querySelector('[data-count]');
    if (counter) counter.textContent = `${shown.length} models`;

    const cov = document.querySelector('[data-quant-cov]');
    if (cov) {
      const withQ = shown.filter((r) => r.quant_retention).length;
      cov.textContent = `${withQ} of ${shown.length}`;
    }
  }

  document.querySelector('[data-worth-controls]')?.addEventListener('change', render);

  const resp = await fetch(`${base}joined/worth_running.json`);
  const data = await resp.json();
  rows = data.rows;
  render();
</script>
